Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Contemporary live-action; Milwaukee grit to Seattle gray; closed cast

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in downtown Milwaukee. Garbage-can lids mark kickball bases in the alley.

(SOUND: distant traffic, a ball thudding off brick)

PETER V.O.
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real Big Brother. Nick.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

MA, older, tired brown eyes, sits near the TV. PETER, mid-twenties, lean, ordinary clothes, hauls grocery bags. NICK fills the doorway — scarred chin and left eyebrow, reddish-brown mess of hair, wrecking-ball presence, burnt-orange energy even standing still.

(SOUND: TV game-show chatter, floorboards creak)

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don’t know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That’s how old people go downhill.

PETER V.O.
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. People moved out of the way.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Nick shoulders through the door. A lifter mid-curl drops a forty-pound bar and steps aside. Nick nods, takes a fifty for warm-ups.

(SOUND: iron plates clank, grunts)

PETER V.O.
I ain’t gonna curl no fifty. That’s okay. Nick is my brother. He’ll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH)

Kids play kickball. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Little MARK freezes. Young Nick steps up to furious MRS. ENGEL.

(SOUND: glass shatter, then silence)

YOUNG NICK
I did it.

PETER V.O.
Ma paid sixty bucks. Nick never told her it was Mark. None of us did.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter in apron, dough on his hands. Through the front glass: sidewalk traffic.

(SOUND: oven hum, street noise)

A skinny girl with short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, fish-symbol necklace, black V-neck, backpack — SIONNA — trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

(SOUND: door chime as another customer enters)

PETER
Want some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. Coffee sometime — you can call me.

She writes a number. Walks away, glances back over her shoulder like memorizing him.

PETER V.O.
It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean covering my feet. I didn’t want to move.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Ma’s pill bottles lined on the counter. She mumbles, glassy-eyed.

(SOUND: fridge hum, pill bottles rattle)

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma, we don’t go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, empty eyes clearing a fraction.

MA
Oh. Right. I am so sorry.

PETER V.O.
I would have to tell Nick. He’d get mad like it was my fault.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on the TV. Nick slaps Peter’s back. Pool balls crack in the next room.

(SOUND: crowd roar on TV, cue ball break)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she’d go crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. I don’t have money for a nursing home.

Nick’s look could punch. Peter pays and leaves.

(MUSIC: low tense bar drone under the TV)

EXT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter ducks in from the cold. Old wood floors, chain-hung lights, classic literature stacks.

(SOUND: hush of pages, distant heater)

He checks out Wuthering Heights with a temporary paper card.

PETER V.O.
Who needed school when everything was free in here?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - PETER'S ROOM - NIGHT

Peter reads in bed. Heathcliff and Catherine. He drifts.

(SOUND: pages turning, wind at the window)

PETER V.O.
Somehow in my dream I mixed up Sionna and Catherine.

EXT. DREAM FOREST AND FIELD - DAY

Stylized, lush. Peter in embroidered red tunic behind Sionna on a dappled mare. Light-blue eyes. First kiss in tall grass.

(MUSIC: soft ethereal strings)

PETER
(bolting awake in bed)
Hold!

(SOUND: gasp, heartbeat)

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, steam wands. Sionna at a back table, green hooded top, watery-blue eyes. Peter sits.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low chatter)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but really loved each other.

SIONNA
I know. I read it. Ghosts. Double characters. The moors. A love that never changes destroys you.

Peter absorbs it like oxygen.

PETER V.O.
Something inside me was coming awake. We made another date. Friday night.

EXT. CITY SIDEWALK - DAY

They walk. Peter’s voice rides the street.

(SOUND: bus brakes, footsteps)

PETER V.O.
Maybe that was Ma’s problem. Dad left and she never moved on. There ain’t no way I could ever forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Peter in T-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face in downward dog. Sionna beside him, calm. Twenty others breathe.

(SOUND: instructor’s soft count, collective exhale)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
I felt so dumb.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

After class he is strangely calm — even at the pizza ovens later, nothing bothers him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Nick stomps. Ma in her chair, blank. Nick’s fists open and close.

(SOUND: door slam echo still in the air, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I met a girl. We had a couple dates.

NICK
A girl. That’s worth all this? Priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

PETER V.O.
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He wouldn’t understand her. She might be afraid.

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON faces Peter. Grey sky in the window.

(SOUND: distant siren, clinic intercom)

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Take her to the ER. Admit her.

PETER
I’ll call Nick.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma dozes. Peter asleep in the chair. Dream: battlefield chaos, blood, steel. A dying man whispers “Save her.” Nick runs in — and drives a knife into Peter’s chest.

(SOUND: dream steel clash, then Peter’s gasp waking)

PETER V.O.
Nick had killed me. Betrayal by the closest family. Worst of all.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill container on the table. Sionna helped fill it. Nick in boots and suede jacket, beet-red, smirking.

(SOUND: TV cop-show theme, boots on floor)

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

PETER
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

PETER
You haven’t even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
You’re so goddamn hateful—

Whack. Nick’s hand across Peter’s head. Ear rings. Door slams.

(SOUND: slap, door crash, ringing in ear)

PETER V.O.
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Months later. Smoke, AC/DC, quarters on the rail. Nick spots ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Rage climbs. Peter steps between.

(SOUND: AC/DC “Have a Drink on Me,” pool-room hush)

PETER
Nick, leave it. You don’t even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You’re next.

Nick’s fist draws back in slow motion. Joe and a bouncer rush in. Head-butt. Chaos. Peter runs into the cold night — all the way to Sionna’s door.

(MUSIC: bar rock cutting to cold wind and heartbeat)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter collapses into her. She strokes his face on the green suede sofa.

(SOUND: distant traffic, her soft breath)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that.

PETER
I love Nick. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don’t let him stop your purpose.

PETER
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you, and honor it.

They sleep. Dream: Sionna rises from a lake as an angel; Nick as giant with mace; her force shrinks him to nothing.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Inevitable meeting. Sionna offers pie. Nick refuses sugar. He sizes her up.

(SOUND: forks on plates, awkward silence)

NICK
University lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
College professor. More women earn degrees now. Diversity helps us learn. You can’t keep a good woman down.

She exits to the kitchen. Nick’s smile is snake-thin.

NICK
(whisper at the door, to Peter)
Take care of Ma.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Murphy’s Law. Rain-slick intersection of Werner and Lowe. Nick’s Trans Am — burnt orange, eagle on the hood — broadside impact. Screech. Metal. Glass.

(SOUND: tires scream, collision boom, then sirens)

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Ancient stone, 1898 plaque. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces shaking Peter.

(SOUND: station buzz, phones)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Your brother Nicholas is in serious condition. Your mother Olivia passed away at the scene. Toxicology pending. Possible alcohol.

Peter’s hands shake on a styrofoam cup.

PETER V.O.
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick unconscious, chest tube, broken femur, bruised. Peter stares through glass, then turns away.

(SOUND: monitors beep, ventilator hiss)

PETER V.O.
No. I’m really not up to seeing him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Empty chair. Empty afghan. Peter breaks — three-year-old bawling, pulling hair.

(SOUND: raw sobs in empty room)

PETER
Die you fucking jackass. Die.

Sionna holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We’ll figure it out.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits maple lid — muted thunk. Marker: Olivia Anne Olsen. 2 Timothy 4:7.

(SOUND: handful of dirt, wind in trees)

PETER V.O.
I have fought the good fight. Yes, Ma. You did.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. Nick yellow-bruised, weak. Tears.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, distant hallway PA)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes. Cops say vehicular manslaughter.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick flares. Peter realizes: tubes and bed — Nick can’t hurt him now.

PETER
Think about it.

Peter walks out.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Nick at the door. Switchblade. He pins Sionna to the wall, blade at collarbone.

(SOUND: cat scuttle, Nick’s ragged breath, her choked squeak)

NICK
You’re the reason I’m going to jail. Break it off. Cash in a week. Or this blade.

Front door bangs open. Peter lunges. Struggle. Blade bites. Red. Nick elbows Peter, wipes the knife on her shirt, flees into dark.

(SOUND: door slam, body hit wall, wet knife sound, running feet)

PETER
Holy shit, Nick—!

He drops beside her, stacks hands on the wound, screams for 911.

(SOUND: sirens approaching, his panicked breath)

INT. HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Blood on Peter’s clothes. Paramedics. Cops. Beeping means heartbeat still.

(SOUND: gurney wheels, monitor beep)

PETER V.O.
If I hadn’t walked in, she would have died. Savior that couldn’t save himself.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - DAY (MONTHS LATER)

Sionna in the rocking chair, blank ocean eyes. Shoulder recovery. Peter opposite.

(SOUND: clock tick, rain on glass)

SIONNA
I can’t feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. Break. Therapist. Mom and Dad’s.

PETER
Stay. Please.

She walks to the bedroom. His fingers slip off the cliff.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - PIKE PLACE - DAY (SEVEN YEARS LATER)

Grey blanket sky. Hoods not umbrellas. PETER, older, steadier, walks past keyboard busker, tourists, original Starbucks line. Tony’s Boy’s Pizza under a medical building.

(SOUND: ferry horn distant, market crowd, light rain)

PETER V.O.
Seven years since my life stopped. Chain of pizzerias. Tony’s name in honor. I am one of them now. A Seattleite.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

Window seat, Puget Sound, ferry. Cinnamon roll like a halo. Memory hits: blood, hallway, her pale face.

(SOUND: ferry wake, restaurant clink)

PETER V.O.
She blamed me in the end. Not the enlightened being I thought. I drifted. Finished the degree. Built this. Still the void. Still the hatred for my brother.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Theme: gratefulness. Peter on the mat.

(MUSIC: soft bowl drone)

PETER V.O.
Shakespeare — things are neither good nor bad, but thinking makes it so. I still feel sorrow for him. DNA demands it.

INT. PETER'S SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Cat BOZO on the couch. Peter dreams: sage on a green road, dirty pizza box.

(SOUND: city hush through rain glass)

SAGE
Stop judging. All things are an illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

Peter wakes smiling, writes it down. Books a flight to Milwaukee.

EXT. OLD KIRK STREET AND PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Brick fortress unchanged. Parlor now Central Cosmetology. Peter meets older BOB in a deli — thicker, graying, sober-eyed.

(SOUND: deli chatter, plate set down)

BOB
You came back. You got out and did something. Proud of you, man.

PETER
I’m here to see Nick. Let the shit go.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD DUPLEX - DAY

Grayer, naked porch. Peter parks, remembers bamboo rockers, Smiley the cat, air kiss.

(SOUND: kids on skateboards, distant dog)

PETER V.O.
I love her so much. I just want her to be happy. Leave her alone. Move on.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. Scrawny, graying NICK sits. Bags under sad eyes. Fifteen minutes.

(SOUND: locks clank, muffled voices, phone pickup)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
I’m here to tell you some things. Just listen.

(SOUND: room tone, one cough somewhere)

PETER
You’re an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

Nick’s eyes shine.

PETER
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. Releases your control forever.

Tears spill down Nick’s cheeks onto the counter.

NICK
You’re so right. I am a huge asshole. You’re more than I ever was.

GUARD
Time’s almost up.

PETER
I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn’t. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. I’m so proud of you.

Guard takes Nick. Peter hangs up, sits with the yellow receiver, light.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

Rolling green hills. Peter drives free. Airport throng. Boarding pass scanned.

(SOUND: highway hum, then terminal murmur, scanner beep)

PETER V.O.
(whisper)
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

He almost smiles. Weight of thirty years gone.

PETER V.O.
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
